<properties>
    <!-- Dev stream only (monkey-dev.jungle): the (:dev) experiments' switches. A release or
         beta build has neither the property nor the code that reads it. -->
    <!-- 0.9.9, GitHub #4: START on the summary's Track page opens the firmware's map over the
         saved track. Experimental — the native map view killed the app during recording on the
         fenix 8 three releases running; this trial runs it only after the session is saved. -->
    <property id="mapAfterSave" type="boolean">false</property>

    <!-- The per-page data-screen editor's properties (dev only since 0.9.16).
         They lived in resources/settings/properties.xml until this round. A release or beta
         build has neither these rows nor the code that reads them: PageModel._store is
         `(:notdev)` there and answers with the default table, which is exactly the page set
         those builds already showed every rider who never opened the list.

         READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING A DEFAULT HERE. A stored property beats this file, and it
         is written on FIRST INSTALL only — an app update leaves whatever is already there
         alone. A watch that carried the release or beta stream and moves to dev therefore
         arrives with NO page properties at all and picks these up; a dev watch that already
         has them keeps what the rider set. -->

    <!-- Feature 1: data screens. Mirror of PageModel.DEF_LAYOUT / DEF_SLOTS; change both
         together, and note which side wins: on a real device these defaults beat the code's,
         so a page changed in only one place ships as the OLD page.
         0.8.0: page 1 is LAYOUT_MAIN (9), the bespoke main screen.
         0.8.2: its giant is slot 1 and defaults to M_BEST_10S (11), not live speed — a rider
         reads the watch when he is NOT moving, and the live number then says nothing about
         the run he just did. Set it to M_SPEED (1) to get the speedometer back. -->
    <!-- 0.9.11: a switch that acts as a button — on = write the seven defaults below back
         into the store and turn this off again (AppSettings.consumeResetPages). -->
    <property id="resetPages" type="boolean">false</property>
    <property id="pg1Layout" type="number">9</property>
    <property id="pg1s1" type="number">11</property>
    <property id="pg1s2" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg1s3" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg1s4" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg1s5" type="number">0</property>
    <!-- Page 2, the FOIL page (layout 10, LAYOUT_FOIL). It replaces the Session grid: a titled
         3x2 table — the two foil shares, the two foil totals, the two bests — under one
         "min / km" pair of column headers. The session odometer and the flight count left it
         (neither is a foil number); both remain catalog metrics for any other slot, and the
         count keeps its own summary screen.

         READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING A DEFAULT HERE. A stored property beats this file: the
         values below are written into a watch's property store on FIRST INSTALL, and an app
         UPDATE leaves the ones already there alone. So a watch that already carries 0.8.2 with
         pg2Layout = 2 keeps the Session grid — with its pair band, which is why the GRID4
         renderer and PageModel.bandPartner are untouched and that page still looks right. Only
         a fresh install picks the foil page up automatically; an existing one gets it by
         setting "Page 2 layout" to "Foil" in Garmin Connect (or by reinstalling).

         The SLOTS below are deliberately left at the old grid's five metrics even though
         LAYOUT_FOIL reads none of them: switching page 2 back to "Grid" then restores exactly
         the page that was there, and pg2s1 = 2 keeps the foil-% bezel arc on the page in
         either layout (PageModel.pageDrawsFoilArc). -->
    <property id="pg2Layout" type="number">10</property>
    <property id="pg2s1" type="number">2</property>
    <property id="pg2s2" type="number">5</property>
    <property id="pg2s3" type="number">6</property>
    <property id="pg2s4" type="number">7</property>
    <property id="pg2s5" type="number">3</property>
    <property id="pg3Layout" type="number">4</property>
    <property id="pg3s1" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg3s2" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg3s3" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg3s4" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg3s5" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg4Layout" type="number">5</property>
    <property id="pg4s1" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg4s2" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg4s3" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg4s4" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg4s5" type="number">0</property>
    <!-- Page 5, TACKS & JIBES (layout 12, LAYOUT_KINDS), new in 0.9.17. It pushed the clock,
         the timeline and the map down one, and the set is eight pages now. READ THE WARNING
         ABOVE: a dev watch that already carries pg5..pg7 keeps the clock, timeline and map it
         has and picks up the NEW key pg8Layout, so its map page arrives twice until it takes
         "Reset pages to defaults" — which writes exactly the eight rows below. A fresh
         install gets the shipped order straight away. -->
    <property id="pg5Layout" type="number">12</property>
    <property id="pg5s1" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg5s2" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg5s3" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg5s4" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg5s5" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg6Layout" type="number">6</property>
    <property id="pg6s1" type="number">8</property>
    <property id="pg6s2" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg6s3" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg6s4" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg6s5" type="number">0</property>
    <!-- Page 7, the TIMELINE (layout 8). It was page 6 until 0.9.17. Slots unused. -->
    <property id="pg7Layout" type="number">8</property>
    <property id="pg7s1" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg7s2" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg7s3" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg7s4" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg7s5" type="number">0</property>
    <!-- Page 8, the breadcrumb MAP (layout 7), page 7 until 0.9.17. It ships ON: the page has
         existed since 0.7 but was never in the default set, and a rider who has to find "Map"
         in a layout list in Garmin Connect never learns it is there. It goes last because it
         is the one page that cannot be drawn into — no PAUSED banner, no state ring — which
         is also why the pager skips it while paused. Slots unused. -->
    <property id="pg8Layout" type="number">7</property>
    <property id="pg8s1" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg8s2" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg8s3" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg8s4" type="number">0</property>
    <property id="pg8s5" type="number">0</property>
</properties>
